        "image": "assets/layered_hairstyles_162_14.jpg",
        "description": "A ghost layers haircut for square face shapes focuses on softening strong angles at the jaw and forehead while adding movement through the lengths. Stylists often avoid blunt cuts that hit exactly at the jawline because they can emphasize squareness, and instead take the length just below or above that point.\n\nGhost layers are built internally, especially around the mid-lengths, to allow the hair to curve and move rather than hang straight and boxy. Face-framing pieces might start around the cheekbones and flow diagonally toward the shoulders, which helps diffuse strong lines and bring a bit of softness to the profile.\n\nBecause the outer edges stay clean, the cut still looks polished and structured, which works nicely with a square jaw, but the movement from ghost layers stops the shape from feeling too rigid. It\u2019s a subtle way to flatter a square face without losing length or resorting to overly choppy, visible layers.",
