          "To keep things flattering, a slightly deeper root shadow or smudge is often left at the scalp so there is a smooth transition from your natural dark base to the lighter champagne lengths. This technique works well for medium to deep skin tones, where the contrast between the root and champagne ends adds dimension without looking too high\u2011contrast or artificial.",
          "Because dark hair can be more prone to damage when bleaching, bond\u2011building treatments in the salon and restorative masks at home are essential. Expect more frequent gloss appointments at first to refine the tone, then you can move to maintenance every few months once the color is established."
